Z||000000||BC-GT||04-02-2001||04:59 PM||||Where to start?<BR>First off, I don't have any of that phantom wiper stuff, everything on the stalk works(delay,mist etc.)just the pump alone doesn't seem to work. When I hook a test light up to it, the bulb pulses, but very very weakly. You can hardly see it lighting at all but it does. Seems like it just not getting quite enough juice. Have 2 different pumps and both are ok when I hot wire them to the battery. Any suggestions where to start looking.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||8
Z||000001||85Josh||04-02-2001||05:13 PM||||Sounds like a bad connection, most likely a ground. Check everything for corrosion, then try again.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000002||BC-GT||04-02-2001||07:30 PM||||All the connections up there look like they did when the thing rolled out of the factory, no rust no corrosion nothing.<BR>where would the ground be?<P>Anyways, the car never made it back from a trip downtown today, started bucking coming up to a light, then died, started right up in park or neutral but wouldn't go into gear without dying. I think it's the torque convertor lockup solenoid. My first tow job, I guess I'm an official member. hahaha <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||85Josh||04-02-2001||09:13 PM||||Grumble,Grumble, I guess I gotta go outside and look! Ok, back now. The black wire is the ground, I'm not sure where it ties into the frame. If you could get a voltmeter onto the connector, I'd bet that you are getting less than 12 volts, maybe as low as 7 or 8. More than likely, you have a loose connection or a broken wire somewhere between the switch and the pump. It is always a PITA to find problems like these, good luck. As to the TCC, I don't know squat about them, never had an automatic newer than an '82!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4<BR>CAVALRY SCOUT!(stuck in Field Artillery)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Carrolles||04-04-2001||08:10 AM||||When you have the proverbial TCC Solenoid sticking problem wait for the engine to cool a bit and reach down between the front of the trans side cover and the exhaust crossover pipe and disconnect the TCC solenoid wiring connector.  I did this on my 85GT on the side of the road without waiting for it to cool.  I did not get burned (well not too badly anyway).  When the trans cools down a bit it will usually release.  You'll then be fine to drive until you can replace it.<P>There is an excellent article with pictures showing what is involved in replacing it.  I downloaded it from the internet but can't remember where I got it from.  It's not hard to do but it is time consuming.  The biggest problem for me was getting all of the old gasket material scraped off the trans case.<P>You'll be working from up top, from down under, and through the driver's side wheel well.  There are about 11 small bolts to remove to get the side cover off.  They're not very difficult to get to.  I had to disconnect the shift cable to make room for the cover to come out the top.  <P>The new solenoid I got had quick connect wire splicers.  I simply snipped the two old wires and crimped on the new ones.  So far so good.  The solenoid cost around $20 and the side cover gasket was around $6.<P>The new solenoid mounting plate did not fit very well and I had to file the bolt hole a bit to get it to bolt up.  I believe I got it from NAPA or Ken's Auto.<P>Hope this helps,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000005||BC-GT||04-04-2001||09:41 PM||||Under 5 minutes before a tow truck swooped down on me(main drag at rush hour)Anyway, had it towed right to the tranny shop, pretty much figured it was the solenoid. Thanks though. <BR>Still need help with the washer pump <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> Only getting like 4 volts to the positive lead?||||reg||1
